WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.902
-Our first guest tonight is
the talented actress you know

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.137
from classic movies
such as "The Breakfast Club"

00:00:05.204 --> 00:00:07.473
and "Pretty in Pink" and
more recently from "The Bear,"

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.008
"Dahmer," and "Riverdale."

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:13.179
She currently is starring in
"Feud: Capote Vs. The Swans,"

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:15.181
which airs Wednesdays
at 10:00 p.m. on FX

00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:16.649
and streams the next day
on Hulu.

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.885
Let's take a look.
-Hi, honey.

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:21.921
Oh, hi.
I come bearing gifts.

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:23.756
-Ooh. Gracias.

00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:26.325
Thank you, thank you.

00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:28.694
-Happy Thanksgiving.
-Mwah! Mwah!

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:31.230
Why is your friend
sitting in the car?

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.767
-John wants me to buy him
a house in Malibu.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:38.638
Little Prince Pauper is pouting.

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.439
Once he realizes
there's a Manhattan

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:43.276
waiting for him,
he'll come in.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:44.978
When's dinner?
-In an hour.

00:00:45.044 --> 00:00:48.080
But there's lots of snacky,
drinky things.

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:50.282
I hope you like
nachos and tamales.

00:00:50.349 --> 00:00:52.217
-I love!
[ Chuckles ]

00:00:52.284 --> 00:00:55.021
-Do you know what that is?

00:00:55.088 --> 00:00:57.890
-Please welcome to the show
Molly Ringwald, everybody.

00:00:57.957 --> 00:00:59.992
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:06.932 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:13.873 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.309
Welcome to the show.
Please join us.

00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:18.811
Take a seat.
-I'm kind of afraid to sit down.

00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:22.615
'Cause I never wear skirts
that are this short.

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:24.084
-Yeah, we can stand.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.284
Do you want to do
a standing interview?

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.619
-No, no, I can do it.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:27.720
-That's great.
-Alright.

00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.689
-Okay. Great.
Congratulations.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:31.290
Thank you for being here.
Thank you for wearing pink.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:34.861
It's very fitting.
-Yes, I'm reclaiming the color.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:36.328
-There you go.
Taking it back.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:37.697
-Yes.
-I will say, you know,

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.966
oftentimes I will meet people
on the show who --

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.700
and I've known their work
over the years,

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.137
but I feel like
I really know you from

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:45.704
when I first saw your films.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:47.807
And I'm sure you have
that all the time.

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.675
-Yes.
-And these are films now

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.710
that your kids --
you have a 20-year-old,

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:53.112
you have 14-year-old twins.
-Yes.

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:54.881
-They're sort of, I mean,
even a little bit older

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.382
than when I first
saw the movies.

00:01:56.449 --> 00:01:57.917
-How old were you?

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:02.221
-I guess when they came out,
maybe I was about 13, as well.

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.656
So, yeah, 12, 13.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:06.892
And how did they
respond to them?

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.161
-Well, I saw the first --

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.531
I saw "The Breakfast Club"
with my daughter,

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:13.800
my 20-year-old,
when she was 10,

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:17.603
which really is too young
to see those movies.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.439
But all of her friends
had seen them.

00:02:20.506 --> 00:02:23.842
And, I mean, because 10 years
old now is a little bit,

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:25.311
I don't know, like --

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:26.746
-Do you think it's older
or younger?

00:02:26.812 --> 00:02:27.981
-I think it's older.
-Yeah.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:31.451
-I think 10 now is
sort of more like 13.

00:02:31.518 --> 00:02:33.386
-I think that's true.
-You know,

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.488
and it was a really
incredible experience.

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:37.423
I was really nervous.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.393
I was really scared that
she wasn't going to like it.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:42.161
-Yeah.
-Um, but she did.

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:45.464
She really liked it,
but a lot of the, you know,

00:02:45.531 --> 00:02:49.135
the more racy stuff, kind of,
the more late-night stuff

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:50.870
kind of went over her head.

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.305
-That's a dream, though.
when you watch stuff with kids

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:55.007
and you realize
it's going over their head

00:02:55.073 --> 00:02:56.976
and you don't have to have
a tricky conversation.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:02:59.379
-Yes, yes.
-Yeah.

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.981
-And then I saw the movie,
actually, just recently,

00:03:02.047 --> 00:03:05.718
a couple weeks ago,
with my 14-year-old twins.

00:03:05.785 --> 00:03:09.689
Uh, and, you know,
I was again really nervous

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.490
that they weren't
going to like it.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:13.826
And they both really liked it.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.227
-That's great.
-You know, they did.

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:16.930
I mean, they didn't
pick up their phones once,

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.597
which I think is a triumph.
-Oh, my God.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.900
-I mean, I was sitting there,
and, you know, I was watching.

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:23.603
The phones were there,
and I was like,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.770
"How long is it going to take?"

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:26.206
You know, but, you know,

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:28.274
but they also took issue
with certain things,

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:30.642
which I've sort of
written about extensively.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:32.211
-Well, right,
some things have --

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:34.581
It's an interesting situation.

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:36.782
Some things have aged
maybe not ideally,

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.017
but also I think

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:40.286
one of the wonderful
things about those films

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:43.556
is when you sort of --
if you can get past that,

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:46.225
it did talk about things
that kids still experience now,

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.128
even though it's, you know, in
some cases almost 40 years later

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:51.564
and not a lot of movies,
I think, address them

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.866
and sort of value
kids' intelligence

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.134
at the level those films did.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:59.972
-Yeah, and they really
do still speak to kids,

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.108
you know, mostly because
really other than,

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:05.511
you know, the Internet,
which is huge,

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.413
it hasn't really
changed anything.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.947
You know,
people are still bullied,

00:04:09.014 --> 00:04:13.486
people still feel out of place
and different

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:16.088
and, you know, and other,
no matter who you are,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.824
and sort of the amazing thing
about "The Breakfast Club,"

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:21.060
it was like
we all feel the same.

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.495
No matter who you are,
you have these feelings.

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:25.632
And that's a really
powerful message for kids.

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.067
-And I think it's nice to know
that you can watch them now.

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.835
And even if it does have --

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.637
or start conversations
with your kids

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:35.074
based on things that maybe
are harder to watch today,

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.076
that is what those movies
always were good at

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:39.378
is having conversations
that maybe parents and kids

00:04:39.444 --> 00:04:40.779
didn't quite know
how to start with each other.

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.248
-Yes, exactly.

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:44.850
Yeah, I think it was maybe
one of the longest conversations

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:48.387
I've had with my 14-year-old
in a while.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.356
-Wow.
So even after the film,

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:51.757
they didn't go straight
to their phones?

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:55.194
-No, no, we sat and debriefed
and talked about things.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:04:56.795
-Did it get so awkward
at some point,

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.364
did you ever say,
like, "You should check.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:59.765
You guys might have some texts.

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:02.502
You should -- You guys want
to take a phone break?

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.370
You know, see what
our friends are up to?

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:05.804
Let's get on Instagram.

00:05:05.871 --> 00:05:08.107
Let's throw some likes
out there."

00:05:08.173 --> 00:05:10.943
-No, no, no.
I was just, like, loving it.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.444
I was just loving it.
-That's great.

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:15.248
This is --
I think one similarity

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:19.051
between this wonderful show
is this is a show

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:20.652
about another clique of people.

00:05:20.719 --> 00:05:22.088
This is an adult clique.

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.756
This is about Truman Capote...
-Yes.

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:25.958
-...and this group of Swans,
as he called them,

00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:28.327
which was, uh --
were a series of socialites

00:05:28.394 --> 00:05:30.563
that were his close friends.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.965
And over the course of the show,
we see that

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:37.269
not a lot of those friendships
maintained over his lifetime.

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:40.439
But you did play the one person
who stayed close to him.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:44.010
-Yeah, I was not --
My character is Joanne Carson,

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:48.380
who for people who don't know
who Joanne Carson is or was,

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.716
she was the second wife
of Johnny Carson,

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.317
who was -- I don't know.

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:54.853
I mean, does everyone still know
who Johnny Carson was?

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.223
-Yeah, I think they do.
I think Johnny Carson -- Yeah.

00:05:57.289 --> 00:05:59.458
Although I do feel like
we might worryingly

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:00.860
be at the end of that.
-Yeah. No.

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:04.430
I mean, my kids -- no idea
who Johnny Carson was,

00:06:04.496 --> 00:06:06.499
but, you know,
so he was this huge, uh --

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:09.134
you know, pretty much
was the head of Hollywood.

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:10.502
I mean, not the head,
but, like,

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:12.471
hugely important in Hollywood.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.407
And Joanne Carson
was his second wife.

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.510
And then they got divorced

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:20.479
and she was sort of
on the outs, you know.

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:23.149
To go from being with the most
important person in Hollywood

00:06:23.215 --> 00:06:26.418
to all of a sudden not,
you know,

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:28.121
she wasn't invited
to the same parties,

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.755
she wasn't a part
of the same group.

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:32.424
And it happened
at around the same time

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:36.795
that Truman Capote had the
big falling-out with the Swans,

00:06:36.862 --> 00:06:40.733
who were Babe Paley
and C.Z. Guest

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:43.502
and Slim Keith
and Lee Radziwill,

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:50.142
who was
Jackie Kennedy Onassis' sister.

00:06:50.208 --> 00:06:52.712
Uh, yeah,
and Joanne was really --

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:54.746
They met in New York,

00:06:54.813 --> 00:06:58.918
but they really became friends
after those events.

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:00.052
-This is a Ryan Murphy show,

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:02.788
and you play
a kind person in this.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:05.791
And you were also in
"Dahmer," Ryan Murphy's show.

00:07:05.857 --> 00:07:08.327
-Also a kind person, yeah.
-A kind person in that.

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.329
Um, I think it's--
I think no one

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.898
has ever had a two
Ryan Murphy show streak

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.466
of playing a nice person.

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:17.403
Do you have a dream if you
collaborated with him again

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.038
of what you would be cast as?
-Yes.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.439
-What would you like to do?

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:21.874
-I would like
to play a psycho bitch.

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.209
-Okay.

00:07:23.275 --> 00:07:25.211
-Am I allowed to say that
on this show?

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:28.380
-You should ask before
you say it, but yes.

00:07:28.447 --> 00:07:30.616
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:32.784
You are allowed -- Not only
are you allowed to say it,

00:07:32.851 --> 00:07:33.853
you're allowed to play it.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.588
And I feel like Mr. Murphy,

00:07:35.655 --> 00:07:37.456
you know,
one of his many strengths,

00:07:37.522 --> 00:07:39.459
he writes a great psycho bitch,
and I feel like --

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.894
-Yeah, I would be --
I would be thrilled

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:45.030
to play something
other than a nice person.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.400
Although I'm really flattered

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:50.102
and I think it's really nice
that clearly he --

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:51.770
he thinks that
I'm a nice person.

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:53.138
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:54.707
But it would be fun
to play something,

00:07:54.773 --> 00:07:58.277
you know, against type.
-Yes. It would be nice.

00:07:58.344 --> 00:08:00.846
So you mentioned --
uh, obviously we mentioned,

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:02.381
I mentioned you're dressed
in pink,

00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:06.385
and I, uh, grew my hair out
during the pandemic.

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.387
And I thought that my goal
was to look like

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:09.855
Steff from "Pretty in Pink."

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:12.992
-That's a good goal.
-Yeah. Now, I came close-ish.

00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:16.896
I ran into a certain hurdle,
which is my current hair

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:19.765
does not have the fullness
of James Spader '80s hair.

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:22.167
-Neither does James Spader.
-Yes.

00:08:22.234 --> 00:08:24.336
You know what?
I will say --

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.605
-Sorry, James.
-Yes.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:29.541
It's true.
I'm closer to it now than he is,

00:08:29.608 --> 00:08:31.743
so I've got that going for me,
but I feel like I did okay.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.112
I mean, not bad.
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:33.178 --> 00:08:34.380
-Really?
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:34.447 --> 00:08:37.183
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:37.249 --> 00:08:39.451
Not bad, right?
-You nailed it.

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:40.852
-I feel really good about it.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.554
-You could have undone
one of those buttons there.

00:08:42.621 --> 00:08:45.223
-Yeah, I also don't have
Spader chest,

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:47.393
but in general --
in general, it worked out.

00:08:47.459 --> 00:08:49.428
Well, thank you so much.
I mean, coming from you,

00:08:49.494 --> 00:08:51.230
that means most of all,
'cause, by the way,

00:08:51.297 --> 00:08:55.034
I showed it to James,
and he was like, "Unh."

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:56.101
-Hilarious.

00:08:56.168 --> 00:08:57.569
-Thanks so much for being here.

00:08:57.636 --> 00:08:58.671
What a pleasure and an honor.

00:08:58.737 --> 00:09:00.139
You guys,
this is Molly Ringwald.

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:02.841
"Feud: Capote Vs. The Swans"

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:04.810
airs Wednesdays
at 10:00 p.m. on FX

00:09:04.876 --> 00:09:06.278
and streams the next day
on Hulu.

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:08.080
Stick around. We'll be
right back with Kay Adams.

